USOS CSMWrap patch 0019 (2026-10-18): USB mouse wheel for more mouse shapes

Patch 0016 keeps a mouse in the boot protocol (no wheel) when any input
report of its interface is longer than one interrupt packet; gaming mice
often carry long vendor reports next to the mouse report. It also sets up
only the first boot HID interface of a device, so a mouse behind a boot
keyboard interface (wireless keyboard/mouse receivers, keyboards with a
pointing device) was not set up at all. With this patch (generic, no
vendor or product IDs):
- With report IDs, input reports of other IDs that are longer than one
  packet are accepted (up to 4 such IDs, up to 127 packets each): a packet
  starting with such an ID is skipped together with its continuation
  packets, counted from the report length. When the report fills its last
  packet exactly, the device may end it with a zero-length packet; the host
  controller then leaves its buffer as it was, so the next packet of that
  mouse, if byte-identical to the previous one, is skipped as well. The
  mouse report itself must still fit in one packet; without report IDs a
  longer report is still refused (nothing tells the reports apart).
- Report descriptors up to 4096 bytes (was 1024), up to 16 input report
  IDs (was 8).
- usb.c: when a device's first boot HID interface is a keyboard, its first
  later boot mouse interface (alternate setting 0) is set up too, with a
  copy of the device record (same default pipe and address). Keyboards are
  set up exactly as before; a second keyboard interface is never added
  (usb-hid.c has one key state). The device counts as configured when the
  keyboard or that mouse was set up.
Mice without a wheel, or whose shape is still refused, stay in the boot
protocol as before; the PS/2 BIOS INT 15h C2xxh behaviour is unchanged.

Own code (HID 1.11 report descriptor format, USB 2.0 5.7.3 / 8.5.3.2
transfer end by short or zero-length packet). Applies after 0001-0010,
0012, 0014-0018, patch -p1 from the top of the tree. Part of a MODIFIED
CSMWrap made by the USOS project (SeaBIOS LGPLv3).

